// tsconfig.tests.json — the TEST-INCLUSIVE typecheck target (27-REVIEW § WR-04).
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `tsconfig.json` turned on `noUnusedLocals` / `noUnusedParameters` (IN-01's
// remedy) but its `exclude` list carries `"**/*.test.ts"`, so `tsc --noEmit --listFiles` reported
// ZERO test files — measured, not assumed. Vitest's transform strips types without checking them.
// The dead-code flags therefore had no reach into the surface this phase actually grew by well over
// a thousand lines. A flag turned on over code it cannot see is a control that reads as enforced and
// enforces nothing.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. It does not replace or loosen the shipped-source target. `tsconfig.json`
// keeps its exclude list byte-unchanged, so `npm run build` and `npm run freshness` keep emitting
// EXACTLY the same committed `.js` set. This target sets `noEmit` and emits nothing at all — the
// only thing it adds is REACH.
//
// The `include` list is inherited from `tsconfig.json` (install/, scripts/, hooks/). The only
// override is the exclude list, which drops the `**/*.test.ts` entry and keeps the other three.
//
// scripts/runnable-ref/fixtures/** IS KEPT. Those files are a parse CORPUS for
// uat-spec-integrity.js, not tooling source: they import @playwright/test (a dependency grugops
// deliberately does not carry) and several of them contain, on purpose, the exact constructs the
// checker refuses. Typechecking a corpus asserts nothing about the checker that reads it.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "exclude": ["node_modules", ".tmp-build", "scripts/runnable-ref/fixtures/**"]
}
